# Dev Environment — `mcp/`

## Everything runs in Docker

Production runs `node:20-alpine` in a pinned image. To avoid host-Node version drift, all dev commands execute inside the same image via `make` (run from `mcp/`).

```bash
cd mcp/

make lint              # eslint --max-warnings 0
make lint-fix          # eslint --fix
make format            # prettier --write
make format-check      # prettier --check
make typecheck         # tsc --noEmit
make test              # vitest run (all suites)
make test-unit         # vitest run tests/unit
make test-integration  # vitest run tests/integration
make test-isolation    # vitest run tests/isolation (mandatory merge gate)
make test-cov          # vitest run --coverage
make check             # everything; ~3 min on first run

# Architecture / quality gates (run inside `make check`)
make check-file-sizes
make check-imports
make check-shared-state
make check-tool-naming

# Security
make audit             # npm audit --audit-level=moderate
make license-check     # license-checker, MIT/Apache/BSD/ISC whitelist
```

**Rules:**

- NEVER run `tsc`, `eslint`, `vitest`, or `node` directly on the host. Use `make`. (You may run them via your IDE for inline feedback; commit-time validation is `make check`.)
- NEVER install project dependencies on host Node. The Docker image owns `node_modules` via a named volume.
- Use `make build` to rebuild the image after dependency changes.

## Dependency management — npm

```bash
make install           # npm ci (or npm install if no lockfile)
# Manual deps still go through docker-compose run
docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml run --rm mcp-dev npm install <pkg>
docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml run --rm mcp-dev npm install --save-dev <pkg>
```

`.npmrc` enforces `save-exact=true`. Always commit `package.json` AND `package-lock.json`.

## Secrets & .env

- `.env` in `mcp/` root, gitignored.
- `.env.example` documents every var with safe defaults.
- The TypeScript `Config` schema (`src/shared/config.ts`, zod-based) is the ONLY way `src/` reads env vars. Direct `process.env.X` outside `Config` is a code-review blocker.

## Logging

- Use the `Logger` port everywhere. NEVER `console.*` (ESLint rejects it).
- `LOG_LEVEL` env var: `trace | debug | info | warn | error | fatal`.
- `LOG_FORMAT`: `json` (prod default) or `pretty` (dev). `pretty` is `pino-pretty` piped from `pino`.

```ts
ctx.logger.info({ tool_name: "list_scans", elapsed_ms: 42, api_status: 200 }, "tool.done");
```

Field names mirror the api repo so cross-system grep just works (see [`mcp-naming-conventions.mdc`](.cursor/rules/mcp-naming-conventions.mdc) § Field names).

## Pre-commit hooks

```bash
make install-hooks   # one-time: installs lefthook
```

Hooks run on `git commit`:

- Prettier on staged TS/JSON/MD files.
- ESLint --fix on staged TS files.
- A quick scan that refuses to commit `.env`, `*.pem`, `id_rsa`, etc.
- commitlint on the commit message.

On `git push`: `tsc --noEmit` + `vitest run tests/unit --no-coverage` (fast feedback before CI).

The full gate runs in CI; hooks are just early-fail.

## IDE

- VS Code / Cursor: install the ESLint and Prettier extensions. The flat config and `.prettierrc.json` Just Work.
- TypeScript "Use Workspace Version" — match the pinned `typescript` in `package.json`, not the IDE's bundled one.
- `.editorconfig` ensures spaces/LF/2-indent consistency across editors.

## Production parity

The Docker image used for `make check` IS the image deployed to `mcp.kaminari.ad`. No "dev vs prod" runtime differences. The only difference is:

- Dev: `command: ["npx", "tsx", "src/bin.ts", "--transport=stdio"]`.
- Prod: `command: ["node", "dist/bin.js", "--transport=http"]`.

Both run inside the same `node:20-alpine` base.
